#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158250} :activity/id #uuid "ecfe7472-c21f-5cb6-9a9a-8377a10cac54" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049786 :topic/id #uuid "13ff200a-f3dd-575f-8362-6dc70510c750" :topic/math-academy-id 2988} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158539 :tutorial/content "The **variance** of a continuous random variable is the expected squared difference between the variable and its expected value.\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the variance is defined as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{S}(x-\\text{E}[X])^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn practice, though, it's easier to use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [X^2] - \\textrm E[X]^2\n$$\n\n \n\nFor example, consider the continuous random variable $X$ with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{x}{4}, \\quad 1 < x < 3\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $\\textrm E[X]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\cdot \\frac{x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{3}}{12}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we compute $\\textrm E[X^2]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{4}}{16}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =5-{(\\frac{13}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "24436a92-f030-567a-aa1c-938371716e3b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4805 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e48a5625-3306-5288-bda8-33105998d690" :step/math-academy-id 29831 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158251}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158251 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157369 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157189 :question/id #uuid "0a870e80-5a73-59d2-997f-bec2d9a947e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-7988"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "697e5bea-20b4-5de0-9992-7a67ee932809" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048747}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430392}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Continuous Random Variable Given a Two-Branch PDF"} :step/id #uuid "4892229b-e20c-58a7-a09b-56a62bb1e09e" :step/math-academy-id 29832 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158252}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158252 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157370 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157190 :question/id #uuid "77bbf810-ec26-5224-9d3e-62de3e0529fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-7990"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c834972a-1ed0-585c-803c-906fcea8cea8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430391}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Continuous Random Variable Given a Three-Branch PDF"} :step/id #uuid "891a6678-f4fa-595a-b179-6c1d5207f1e4" :step/math-academy-id 29834}] :activity/title "Variance of Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157534}]}
